\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For $A\subset\mathbb R^2$, its diameter is $\sup\{|x-y|:x,y\in A\}$. A convex universal cover is a convex planar set $K$ containing an isometric copy of every set $A$ of diameter one. An isometry may translate, rotate or reflect $A$. Area means two-dimensional Lebesgue measure.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Find a convex universal cover of minimum area and determine that minimum. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.8217}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
What is the smallest area of a convex planar set that can contain, after a rigid motion, every planar set of diameter one?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 7200072 (AMR-071-0072). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Philip Gibbs, A New Slant on Lebesgue’s Universal Covering Problem (2014); arXiv:1401.8217. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.8217}.
\end{description}
